================================================================================
2026-11-22 — Graph / Build / Objects: work-list styling
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Objects tab uses the shared Databricks work-list style
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Context:
The Objects tab (Graph → Build → Objects) rendered plain tables without column
headers, with coloured kind pills and an inline column width. The user asked for
the usual Databricks list styling. The app's Work-List template (filter bar +
.ob-runs-table, as in Build → Runs) is the reference, so the Objects tab now
follows it.

Changes:
1. src/front/templates/partials/dtwin/_build_objects.html
   - Filter bar uses the work-list layout: search box (.ob-filter-search),
     row count (.ob-runs-count) and trailing actions. The subtitle line is
     removed. The Reload button is renamed Refresh.
2. src/front/static/query/js/query-build-objects.js
   - Each table has a sentence-case header row (tableHead): Kind, Name, State
     and an actions column; by-usage adds Source.
   - Kind is plain text. Coloured kind badges (KIND_BADGES) and the inline
     90px width are removed; group location and usage counts are muted text.
   - The shared ObListFilter filters the rows across all group tables and
     drives the row count. It is re-applied after every render, including the
     notice and error states, which now clear the content.
3. src/front/static/global/js/list-filter.js
   - Counts and filters body rows only (tbody > tr), so header rows of a
     multi-table container are never counted or hidden. Existing callers pass a
     tbody and are unaffected.
4. tests/units/front/test_build_objects_tab_contract.py
   - Checks the work-list markup, header rows, no filled badges or inline widths,
     filter binding and the body-row selector.

Not changed:
- Settings → Objects tables still use table-light and the ob-table class, which
  has no CSS rule. They are the older style and are left as they are.

Modified files:
- src/front/templates/partials/dtwin/_build_objects.html
- src/front/static/query/js/query-build-objects.js
- src/front/static/global/js/list-filter.js
- tests/units/front/test_build_objects_tab_contract.py
